    {
      "id": "focus-model-instructions",
      "type": "markdown",
      "label": "Reading This Idea",
      "position": {
        "x": -220,
        "y": 980
      },
      "width": 760,
      "height": 300,
      "ports": [
        {
          "id": "root",
          "label": "root",
          "direction": "bidirectional",
          "dataType": "any",
          "angle": 210
        },
        {
          "id": "top",
          "label": "top",
          "direction": "bidirectional",
          "dataType": "value",
          "angle": 270
        },
        {
          "id": "right",
          "label": "right",
          "direction": "bidirectional",
          "dataType": "value",
          "angle": 0
        },
        {
          "id": "bottom",
          "label": "bottom",
          "direction": "bidirectional",
          "dataType": "value",
          "angle": 90
        },
        {
          "id": "left",
          "label": "left",
          "direction": "bidirectional",
          "dataType": "value",
          "angle": 180
        }
      ],
      "visible": true,
      "showLabel": true,
      "data": {
        "identity": {
          "graphId": "focus-model"
        },
        "title": "Reading This Idea",
        "markdown": "## Focus is orthogonal to zoom\n\nThis idea treats **zoom** and **focus** as separate inputs. Zoom answers *how far away is the viewport?* Focus answers *what is the user working on?*\n\nThe pre-launch target is intentionally small: one authoritative focus state, a way to classify a node's relationship to that focus, and a presentation resolver that can promote focused content without forcing the entire graph to the same semantic level.\n\nSelection, hover, editor-open state, and persistence may interact with focus later, but they should not be silently conflated with it."
      }
    },
